            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What happens to a tenant in common when they die?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "When a tenant in common dies, their ownership share passes through their estate rather than transferring to the surviving co-owners. The deceased owner's fractional interest transfers according to the terms of their will, a trust, or statutory state intestacy laws, and the inherited interest generally receives a stepped-up fair market value basis under 26 U.S.C. 1014(a)(1)."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What happens to a joint tenant when they die?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "When a joint tenant dies, their ownership interest extinguishes immediately and passes automatically to the surviving joint tenants by right of survivorship. The surviving owners' adjusted tax basis is calculated under 26 U.S.C. 1014 and 2040 based on marital status and past financial contributions."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Which is better for taxes, tenants in common or joint tenancy?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Neither title structure is an inherent tax winner, because the federal tax outcome depends on marriage status, acquisition funding, and community property rules. One-half of a qualified joint interest held by spouses is included in the decedent's gross estate under 26 U.S.C. 2040(b), non-spouse joint tenants are evaluated based on financial contributions under Section 2040(a), and community property couples follow the basis rule in 26 U.S.C. 1014(b)(6)."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What are the disadvantages of joint tenancy?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The primary disadvantage of joint tenancy is that the right of survivorship removes your ability to leave your property share to chosen heirs through a will. Furthermore, non-spouse joint owners face strict financial contribution tracing under 26 U.S.C. 2040(a) to avoid having the entire asset included in a deceased owner's gross estate, and individual state statutes such as Florida Section 689.15 require precise deed language to create survivorship rights."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Which is better, tenants in common or joint tenancy?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Joint tenancy is better for co-owners who want their property share to pass to surviving owners under the survivorship right, whereas tenancy in common is better for co-owners who hold unequal stakes or want their share to pass to designated children or heirs. State statutory defaults and estate planning goals dictate which title form functions best for your situation."
                }
              }
            ]
