      "semantic": {
        "text": "ModernWallet Free Financial Calculators Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Commercial Mortgage CD Calculator Emergency Fund Debt Consolidation Inflation Expense Ratio Social Security Break-Even Mileage Reimbursement Portfolio Rebalancing Hurricane Deductible Annuity Credit Card Interest Loan Payment HYSA vs CD Trump Account vs 529 Mortgage Recast Social Security COLA Biweekly Mortgage Roth vs Traditional 401(k) Pay Off Mortgage or Invest Florida Amendment 3 Student Loan Garnishment Tax Bracket Debt-to-Income Ratio Taxable Social Security Checklists Charitable Donation Deduction Salary Car Loan Interest Deduction Paycheck Rent vs Buy Rule of 78 Stock Capital Gains Tax Hidden Cost of Owning a Home × ModernWallet / Compare / SSDI vs SSI SSDI vs SSI Differences in Work Credits and Limits By Jonathan Velez , Personal Finance Writer & Editor · Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 23, 2026 Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) pays monthly benefits to disabled workers who earned enough work credits, while Supplemental Security Income (SSI) pays monthly benefits to aged, blind, or disabled adults with limited income and financial resources. SSDI depends on work credits. SSI depends on being 65 or older, blind or disabled, plus income and resource limits. Confirm current amounts and your own eligibility with the Social Security Administration . On This Page SSDI vs SSI at a Glance Who Qualifies for SSDI Who Qualifies for SSI How Disability Is Defined Can You Get SSDI and SSI Together Who Should Apply for Which Program Our Takeaway for SSDI vs SSI SSDI vs SSI at a Glance The fundamental difference between Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) and Supplemental Security Income (SSI) centers on how an individual qualifies for support. SSDI provides disability benefits to eligible insured workers. Entitlement depends on having enough social security earnings to be insured for disability. In contrast, SSI operates as a needs-based program under Title XVI of the Social Security Act for people who are aged, blind or disabled with limited income and resources. Federal regulations establish distinct eligibility boundaries for each program through the Electronic Code of Federal Regulations (eCFR). The SSDI eligibility rules in 20 CFR 404.315 and 20 CFR 404.130 contain no resource limit. Conversely, SSI eligibility governed by 20 CFR 416.202 requires claimants to have limited income and resources. To examine updated payment baselines and adjustments for both systems, check our guides on the 2027 SSDI COLA and 2027 SSI amounts . Some people receive both SSDI and SSI, as our 2027 SSDI COLA guide notes, citing SSA. When an individual receives both benefits, the Social Security Administration (SSA) treats Social Security and disability benefits as unearned income under 20 CFR 416.1121 . SSDI SSI Basic Program Purpose Provides disability benefits to workers who have enough social security earnings to be insured for disability under 20 CFR 404.315 . Provides monthly financial assistance to aged, blind, or disabled individuals with limited income and resources under 20 CFR 416.202 . Work History Requirement Requires disability insured status achieved through quarters of coverage as specified in 20 CFR 404.130 . The SSI requirements listed in 20 CFR 416.202 do not include work credits. Financial Resource Limits The SSDI eligibility rules in 20 CFR 404.315 and 20 CFR 404.130 contain no resource limit. Restricts countable nonexcludable resources to $2,000 for an individual and $3,000 for an individual and spouse under 20 CFR 416.1205 . Earned and Unearned Income Limits The SSDI entitlement rules in 20 CFR 404.315 and 20 CFR 404.130 contain no income test. Disability is defined by the inability to do substantial gainful activity ( 20 CFR 404.1505 ). Counts earned and unearned income monthly under 20 CFR 416.1100 ; excess income reduces or eliminates monthly payments. Medical Disability Standard Requires a medically determinable impairment preventing substantial gainful activity lasting at least 12 months or resulting in death under 20 CFR 404.1505 . Applies the identical adult disability definition requiring severe impairment lasting at least 12 months under 20 CFR 416.905 . Mandatory Waiting Period Imposes a mandatory 5-month waiting period from disability onset under 20 CFR 404.315 , with exceptions for prior disability within 5 years or ALS. 20 CFR 416.202 does not list a waiting period. Age Requirements Covers workers prior to attaining full retirement age as defined in 20 CFR 404.409 under 20 CFR 404.315 . Covers qualified individuals aged 65 or older, or individuals of any age who are blind or disabled under 20 CFR 416.202 . Concurrent Benefit Potential Workers can receive both benefits if their SSDI payment falls below federal income thresholds under 20 CFR 416.1121 . Recipients can receive both benefits, but SSA counts SSDI as unearned income against monthly SSI payments under 20 CFR 416.1121 . 📣 Building in finance? Put your product in front of readers comparing options on this page. Advertise With Us → Who Qualifies for SSDI Entitlement to SSDI requires meeting precise federal insurance benchmarks established under 20 CFR 404.315. First, an applicant must have accumulated enough social security earnings to be insured for disability, as described in 20 CFR 404.130. Second, the worker must submit an official application for benefits before reaching full retirement age. Third, the individual must have a qualifying disability defined under 20 CFR 404.1505, or have experienced a disability that ended within the 12-month period before the month of filing. Fourth, the applicant must satisfy a mandatory waiting period of five full consecutive calendar months. Disability insured status is governed by four specific rules detailed in 20 CFR 404.130. Under Rule I, commonly referred to as the 20/40 requirement, a worker must be fully insured and possess at least 20 quarters of coverage (QCs) earned within the 40-quarter period ending with the quarter in which disability began. Federal regulations provide alternative eligibility pathways for younger workers who experience disabling medical conditions before accumulating forty calendar quarters. Under Rule II of 20 CFR 404.130, an individual who becomes disabled before age 31 satisfies the insured status test by holding quarters of coverage in at least one-half of the calendar quarters beginning after age 21 and ending with the quarter of disability onset. If that calculation yields an odd number of quarters, the agency reduces the requirement by one quarter. For short periods under 12 quarters, the applicant must show at least six quarters of coverage within the 12-quarter period ending with the disability quarter. Rule III governs individuals who experienced an earlier disability period before age 31 and become disabled again at age 31 or older. Rule IV applies to individuals who are statutorily blind, requiring only that the applicant be fully insured. The mandatory five-month waiting period under 20 CFR 404.315(a)(4) begins with the first full calendar month in which the worker was both insured for disability and medically disabled. By federal rule, this waiting period can begin no earlier than the 17th month before the month the worker files an application. The statute outlines two narrow exceptions where no waiting period is required: if the applicant was previously entitled to disability benefits or a period of disability within five years of the month they became disabled again, or if the individual has been medically determined to have amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) with an application approved on or after July 23, 2020. Who Qualifies for SSI SSI operates under distinct statutory eligibility criteria set forth in 20 CFR 416.202. A claimant must meet all of the requirements in 20 CFR 416.202 to obtain monthly assistance under Title XVI. The individual must be aged 65 or older, blind, or disabled. The applicant must reside within the United States and hold status as a United States citizen, a national, a lawfully admitted permanent resident alien, an alien permanently residing under color of law, or a qualified overseas military dependent. The claimant cannot exceed statutory monthly income ceilings, cannot hold resources beyond strict regulatory caps, cannot be fleeing felony prosecution or confinement, cannot be violating probation or parole, and must formally submit an application for benefits. Income rules codified in 20 CFR 416.1100 establish that countable income serves as the primary mechanism for determining monthly eligibility and benefit calculations. The agency counts income on a monthly basis, distinguishing between earned wages from active employment and unearned income derived from other programs. Federal rules state that the more countable income a claimant receives, the less their monthly SSI benefit will be. If an applicant has too much income under 20 CFR 416.1100, they are not eligible for a benefit. Resource caps established in 20 CFR 416.1205 limit the property and liquid savings a claimant may own. The regulation provides a historical schedule of resource limits in paragraph (c) ending with the row effective January 1, 1989. Under this standard, countable nonexcludable resources cannot exceed $2,000 for an individual and $3,000 for an individual and spouse. Under 20 CFR 416.1201, resources are defined as cash, liquid assets, or real and personal property that an individual owns and could convert to cash to pay for basic support and maintenance. Claimants whose countable resources exceed these regulatory limits are not eligible for benefits. Our guide on SSI and inheritance covers how an inheritance affects SSI. How Disability Is Defined Although SSDI and SSI serve different financial groups, both programs apply the exact same statutory definition of adult disability. Under 20 CFR 404.1505 for SSDI and 20 CFR 416.905 for SSI, the law defines adult disability as the inability to do any substantial gainful activity by reason of any medically determinable physical or mental impairment which can be expected to result in death or which has lasted or can be expected to last for a continuous period of not less than 12 months. Federal examiners assess whether the claimant's documented conditions prevent them from performing their past relevant work under 20 CFR 404.1560(b) or 20 CFR 416.960(b). If an applicant cannot perform past duties, examiners evaluate residual functional capacity along with vocational factors, including age, formal education, and acquired job skills, to establish whether they can adapt to any other substantial gainful work that exists in the national economy. Both regulations specify separate medical evaluation standards for statutory blindness under 20 CFR 404.1581 and 20 CFR 416.981. The adult disability definition uses identical language in both parts of the Code of Federal Regulations, and SSA decides each application. Can You Get SSDI and SSI Together Concurrent eligibility permits an individual to receive benefits from both SSDI and SSI at the same time, provided they satisfy the distinct statutory requirements of each program. An applicant must possess sufficient quarters of coverage under 20 CFR 404.130 to qualify for SSDI, while simultaneously meeting the strict income limitations of 20 CFR 416.1100 and resource ceilings of 20 CFR 416.1205 to qualify for SSI. The interaction between concurrent benefits is governed by federal unearned income rules established in 20 CFR 416.1121. Under paragraph (a), Social Security insurance benefits and disability payments are explicitly classified as unearned income for the purposes of SSI accounting. When an individual is awarded SSDI, the monthly insurance disbursement does not disqualify the worker from SSI automatically. Instead, SSA treats the SSDI check as unearned income, which directly reduces the individual's monthly SSI benefit amount. Our 2027 SSI amounts guide, citing SSA, notes that the Social Security payment rises in January 2027 while the SSI payment rises with the December 31, 2026 payment. Receiving SSDI counts against the SSI income test, because 20 CFR 416.1100 says that if you have too much income you are not eligible for an SSI benefit. Who Should Apply for Which Program Determining which program to pursue depends on your individual employment history, current financial assets, and household earnings. If you may have enough work credits (Rule I in 20 CFR 404.130 asks for at least 20 quarters of coverage in the 40-quarter period ending with the quarter of disability), SSDI is the program to look at first, and you can start through the Social Security Administration Disability Portal . The SSDI eligibility rules in 20 CFR 404.315 and 20 CFR 404.130 contain no resource limit. If you have limited or discontinuous employment history, or if you have never worked in covered employment, you should apply for SSI through the Social Security Administration SSI Portal . The SSI requirements in 20 CFR 416.202 are age, blindness or disability, residency, income, resources and an application. However, you must be prepared to verify your financial standing continuously. Claimants must maintain nonexcludable assets beneath the $2,000 individual ceiling set in 20 CFR 416.1205. This guidance is not intended for individuals whose countable income or liquid assets exceed SSI statutory ceilings and who also lack sufficient quarters of coverage for SSDI. If your resources exceed $2,000 and your work history does not meet the 20/40 rule under 20 CFR 404.130, neither program may provide monthly benefits. In addition, this review does not cover medical standard differences for children. Several legal and factual changes would modify this analysis. A change to the resource limits in 20 CFR 416.1205 would change who qualifies for SSI. Finally, gaining or losing quarters of coverage changes whether an individual must rely on needs-based assistance or insured worker benefits. Claimants must confirm their eligibility directly with the Social Security Administration . Our Takeaway for SSDI vs SSI Apply for SSDI if you are disabled and have enough work credits under 20 CFR 404.130, or apply for SSI if you are 65 or older, blind or disabled and your income and resources are within the limits in 20 CFR 416.202. Some people qualify for both programs, and SSA decides after counting income and resources. 20 CFR 416.1205 lists SSI resource limits of $2,000 for an individual and $3,000 for an individual and spouse, so confirm current amounts with SSA. Compare your work credits and your income and resources against the SSDI vs SSI rules above, then confirm with the Social Security Administration . Frequently Asked Questions What is the main difference between SSDI and SSI? The main difference is that SSDI requires a qualifying work history with paid payroll taxes under 20 CFR 404.315, whereas SSI is a needs-based program requiring limited income and resources under 20 CFR 416.202 regardless of your work record. Can you get SSDI and SSI at the same time? Some people do, but SSA counts Social Security and disability benefits as unearned income for SSI under 20 CFR 416.1121, so the SSDI amount counts against the SSI income test, and SSA decides. Does SSDI have a resource limit? The SSDI eligibility rules in 20 CFR 404.315 and 20 CFR 404.130 contain no resource limit. SSI does have one: 20 CFR 