            "@context": "https://schema.org",
            "@type": "FAQPage",
            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Are stocks safer than bonds?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No — stocks are riskier than investment-grade bonds. Stocks can lose 30–50% in a downturn. High-quality bonds (U.S. Treasuries, investment-grade corporates) rarely lose more than 5–10% in any single year, though they can in extreme rate environments."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Can stocks and bonds both lose money at the same time?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Yes, and 2022 was a stark example. Both the S&P 500 (−18%) and the U.S. bond market (−13%) fell sharply as the Federal Reserve raised rates aggressively. This correlation had been rare historically but reminds investors that diversification across asset classes does not guarantee protection in all conditions."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is a good stocks-to-bonds ratio?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "A common rule of thumb is to subtract your age from 110 to get your stock percentage. At 40, that would be 70% stocks / 30% bonds. Adjust based on your personal risk tolerance and when you need the money — aggressive savers with 30+ years ahead often hold 90%+ in stocks."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Do bonds protect against stock market crashes?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "In most crashes, yes. During the 2008 financial crisis, stocks fell 37% while U.S. Treasury bonds gained about 25%. However, in 2022 both fell together because the cause of the crash (soaring inflation and rapid rate hikes) hurt bonds as much as stocks."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Are stocks or bonds better right now?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "There's no single answer that holds for everyone at every moment — it depends on your time horizon, not on trying to time the market. If you won't need the money for 5+ years, stocks' higher long-run average return (about 10%/year historically) usually wins out despite the volatility. If you need income, stability, or you're within a few years of needing the money, bonds' lower risk and steadier payments matter more. The 110-minus-your-age rule above is a starting point for blending both rather than picking one outright."
                }
              }
            ]
